{
  "markdown_report": "### Insights and framework\n\nThree starting-point insights guide the proposal. First, SASRec may mix recent and older interests; explicit recent evidence could reduce intent dilution. Second, isolated interactions establish neither equipment ownership nor replacement needs; behavioral transitions cannot verify compatibility. Third, Electronics has 14.95% wrong sampled rankings and a −0.4812 lower-tail margin, motivating experiments without implying validation gains.\n\nThree literature insights sharpen the design. [GaGNN](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ins.2024.120623) motivates training-derived relationships; [LSIDN](https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.14743) motivates separate preference timescales, with uncertain sparse-history transfer. [gSASRec](https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/939) motivates loss controls; larger margins alone need not improve ranking. [FPMC](https://archives.iw3c2.org/www2010/pub/pdfs/RendleFreudenthaler2010-FPMC.pdf) and [FOSSIL](https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.09152) establish that additive preference/transition fusion has substantial precedent.\n\nThe conceptual map retains three directions: **temporal evidence** (recent/sustained interests), **relational evidence** (observed transitions/broad associations), and **optimization** (negative coverage/false-negative exposure). A methodological gap crosses them: target-inclusive transition statistics can create shortcuts, analogous to [target-edge leakage](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.00899).\n\n### Candidate assessment\n\nEach cell gives score/10, strength (+), and limitation (−). Difficulty increases with effort.\n\n| Idea | Originality | Potential | User alignment | Model alignment | Difficulty |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Recent/history pooling gate [LSIDN] | 3; +compact; −established | 6; +reusable; −sparse evidence | 7; +intent; −compatibility unknown | 5; +context; −indirect effect | 3; +small branch; −writer changes |\n| **Cross-fitted recent transition residual** [GaGNN, FPMC] | 3; +leakage control; −established fusion | 6; +auditable; −limited pair coverage | 7; +specific next steps; −ownership unproven | 6; +discrimination; −unseen pairs | 5; +sparse operations; −cross-fitting parity |\n| Sixteen-negative sampled softmax [gSASRec] | 2; +clean control; −established | 7; +strong baseline; −protocol dependence | 4; +ranking; −intent implicit | 8; +confusion; −false negatives | 3; +simple loss; −extra scoring |\n\n### Selected idea: recent transition residual\n\nAt 0% progress, retain this reversible extension, beginning with fixed fusion before learning a gate. It targets **broad relevance outranking recent intent** and **unsupported upgrades** through specific independently observed next steps. The latter remains an indirect hypothesis: transitions cannot establish compatibility or satisfied needs. It also targets Electronics’ weak separation tail.\n\nBuild adjacent transition counts from training histories. Use five user-grouped folds so training features exclude the target user’s entire fold. Smooth each row toward a training-derived item-frequency prior; retain its dense prior contribution outside stored edges. Use fixed itemwise transforms, never candidate-set normalization. Start without popularity correction; test clipped correction separately because it amplifies rare-edge noise.\n\n```text\nfit SASRec with its baseline loss\nbuild five complementary training graphs and one deployment graph\nscore(prefix,item) = neural_score + alpha * log1p(support(prefix,item)/tau)\nselect fixed fusion constants using pinned macro validation HR@10\nonly then test an evidence gate or 16-negative softmax separately\n```\n\nCross-fitting reduces self-label shortcuts but does not enforce global chronology; retain pinned splits and test chronological training-feature construction separately. Compare unchanged SASRec, transition-only, recency item-kNN, and fixed fusion before gating. Ablate direction, recency, correction, and count features. Report support coverage and supported/unsupported validation HR@10, plus Electronics’ margin tail. Preserve all four routes, seeds, diagnostic interfaces, and identical full-catalog scoring. Sampled HR@10 does not establish full-catalog gains ([Rendle](https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.02263)); no improvement is claimed before evaluation.",
  "idea": {
    "description": "Add a cross-fitted recent directed-transition residual to per-dataset SASRec. Construct support from adjacent training interactions, shrink sparse rows toward a training-derived popularity prior, and combine it with neural scores using candidate-independent transforms. Establish fixed-weight fusion before testing a small evidence gate.",
    "motivation": "The user diagnosis identifies broad associations outranking recent intent and upgrades lacking supporting evidence. Independent users’ observed next steps offer a testable, indirect signal for both failures, while providing no proof of ownership, compatibility, replacement need, or satisfaction. Electronics’ 14.95% wrong-ranking rate and negative lower-tail margin motivate testing improved candidate discrimination. Count features require cross-fitting because inclusive singleton transitions otherwise encode their own training labels.",
    "implementation_notes": "Retain existing preprocessing, all four dataset routes, preprocessing seed 0, training seed 42, DATA_DIR=os.environ['GAGC_DATA_DIR'], and SASREC_EPOCHS environment handling with host default 200. Write every artifact beneath the trial directory and run on one GPU. Use only permitted training interactions; titles/descriptions are optional for later work, while ratings, reviews, categories, prices, stores, and simulator traits are unavailable.\n\nFirst train unchanged SASRec using its baseline loss, avoiding a simultaneous objective change. Assign training users deterministically to five folds using seed 42. For each fold, construct adjacent directed counts and item-frequency priors using only users outside that fold; build a separate deployment graph from all permitted training histories. Cross-fitted training examples must use their complementary graph, including its prior. This excludes the example’s own target contribution but does not enforce global chronology. Other users’ later training events can remain present; investigate chronological training-feature graphs only as an explicitly separate experiment without altering benchmark splits.\n\nFor a graph with counts C, let n[a]=sum_i C[a,i], f[i] be training item frequency, and p0[i]=f[i]/sum_j f[j] over nonpadding items. Define P(i|a)=(C[a,i]+lambda*p0[i])/(n[a]+lambda). Use the last W nonpadding prefix items, with recency weights proportional to rho^k and normalized over available anchors. Then q(H,i)=sum_k w_k P(i|a_k). For empty prefixes use p0; zero-outgoing-count anchors also yield p0. Store each row as sparse corrections C[a,i]/(n[a]+lambda) plus its scalar prior coefficient lambda/(n[a]+lambda). This preserves contributions to destinations absent from sparse rows. Initial CSR should retain all observed edges; top-K truncation, if needed, is an explicit approximation requiring a separate validation check and retained prior contribution.\n\nUse s(H,i)=z_SASRec(H,i)+alpha*log1p(q(H,i)/tau), with tau=max(1/item_count,1e-12). This fixes neural temperature at one to avoid redundant scale tuning. Scores must be identical for an item regardless of which other candidates are supplied. Do not use candidate-set min–max normalization, z-scores, or separately normalized probability mixtures. Start with W=3, rho=0.7, lambda=10, and alpha=0; these are reproducible initial settings, not evidence-backed optima. Predeclare a small validation sweep alpha in {0,0.1,0.3,1.0}, sharing the same baseline checkpoint. If fusion is promising, separately compare lambda in {1,10,100}, W in {1,3}, and rho in {0.7,1.0}, avoiding an exhaustive joint search. Choose by the pinned four-category macro validation HR@10, break ties toward alpha=0 and then the simpler configuration, and log every evaluated configuration and GPU time within the shared 43,200 GPU-second budget.\n\nFixed fusion can be validated directly without learning from graph-derived training features. Cross-fitting becomes mandatory when training the proposed gate or adapting neural parameters with the residual. For a gate experiment, initially freeze SASRec. Let g(H)=sigmoid(b+w^T e(H)) and replace alpha with alpha*g(H). Prefix-only features are log1p(history length), the recency-weighted log1p outgoing count of anchors, and agreement defined as the weighted fraction of anchor pairs sharing their highest-count outgoing destination; assign zero agreement when fewer than two anchors have outgoing edges. Fit feature means/scales on cross-fitted training prefixes only; use scale one for constant features. Initialize w=0 and b=-4. Candidate identities and validation targets must not enter gate inputs. Optimize baseline loss on cross-fitted scores, compare against the selected fixed residual, and serialize deployment graph statistics and the gate. Cross-fitting still creates a graph-size/support shift at deployment; compare feature distributions and do not claim it guarantees generalization.\n\nStart without popularity correction. As a separate ablation, multiply q by clip((median_positive_frequency/max(f[i],1))^beta,0.5,2.0), with beta in {0,0.25}; recompute all transforms identically in training and inference. Test sixteen uniformly sampled negatives with sampled-softmax loss separately from graph/gate changes, excluding padding, target collisions, and known training-history positives. Unobserved items remain uncertain negatives. Larger margins and higher training support are insufficient selection criteria.\n\nControls should include unchanged SASRec, transition-only scoring, recency-weighted item-kNN, fixed residual fusion, and then the gate. Ablate direction using symmetrized counts, recency using equal anchor weights, popularity correction, and the count feature itself. Select practical controls before optional experiments to conserve budget. For the same sampled validation users/candidates, report raw transition coverage defined by any recent anchor having C[a,target]>0, supported/unsupported HR@10 with subgroup counts, inclusive versus cross-fitted training support, and Electronics’ margin tail. Subgroup metrics are explanatory and never replace macro HR@10.\n\nThe predictor writer must serialize the trained architecture, graph CSR/prior coefficients, fixed constants, gate and feature-standardization parameters, routing metadata, and item mappings beneath the trial directory. Score the complete unseen catalog in chunks and maintain global top-k; neural-only retrieval followed by reranking can omit fused winners. Preserve the baseline’s unseen-item filtering. Independently evaluated working/predict.py and diagnostic scoring must reproduce the same score formula.\n\nKeep DiagnosisProbe(view, device).run_full_diagnosis(train_loader), returning metrics and metric_definitions for batches (None, sequence, positive, negative) of shape B x L. view.model must be the actual deployed nn.Module, including gate parameters and registered scoring buffers where appropriate. Preserve view.item_emb, log2feats(sequence), predict_score(features, items), user_train, meta_data, and review_data. A feature container may hold neural states and prefix anchors, but adapt all probe consumers consistently. For training diagnostics, route reconstructed prefixes through cross-fitted graphs using deterministic prefix-to-fold metadata; exclude ambiguous duplicate prefixes from that diagnostic and report their count. Deployment scoring always uses the all-training graph. State clearly that embedding similarity measures the SASRec embedding component, while margin diagnostics measure combined scores. Missing category breakdowns must be marked unavailable; never infer unavailable categories or reviews. Continue supplying twenty real training users, five per category, with full-catalog unseen recommendations. Validation improvements remain specific to the pinned 200-users/category, 99-unique-negatives, evaluation-seed-42 protocol and do not demonstrate full-catalog gains.",
    "pseudocode": "For each dataset independently:\n    train baseline SASRec with original seeds, splits, and environment defaults\n    assign users to five deterministic folds\n    for fold r:\n        graph[r] = adjacent_counts_and_item_prior(users outside fold r)\n    deployment_graph = adjacent_counts_and_item_prior(all training users)\n\nsupport(graph, prefix, item):\n    anchors = last W nonpadding prefix items\n    if anchors empty: return graph.p0[item]\n    weights = normalize([rho**k for k in recency order])\n    return sum_k weights[k] * (\n        graph.C[anchors[k], item] / (graph.n[anchors[k]] + lambda)\n        + lambda * graph.p0[item] / (graph.n[anchors[k]] + lambda)\n    )\n\nscore(prefix, item, graph):\n    q = support(graph, prefix, item)\n    residual = log1p(q / max(1 / item_count, 1e-12))\n    coefficient = alpha\n    if optional_gate_enabled:\n        evidence = standardized_prefix_only_features(prefix, graph)\n        coefficient *= sigmoid(b + dot(w, evidence))\n    return SASRec_score(prefix, item) + coefficient * residual\n\nFixed-fusion experiment:\n    freeze baseline checkpoint\n    evaluate preregistered alpha values using deployment_graph\n    choose using pinned four-category macro validation HR@10\n\nOptional gate experiment:\n    initialize w=0, b=-4; initially freeze SASRec\n    for each training user's causal prefixes:\n        graph = graph[user_fold]\n        fit baseline loss using combined positive/negative scores\n    validate with deployment_graph\n\nSeparate controls:\n    compare transition-only, item-kNN, direction/recency/correction ablations\n    test 16-negative sampled softmax without silently combining changes\n    record subgroup coverage, margins, macro HR@10, and resource use\n\nSerialize complete scoring state and update predictor writer.\nFor final full-catalog recommendations:\n    use deployment_graph\n    score every unseen candidate in chunks with exactly the same itemwise formula\n    maintain global top-k",
    "originality": {
      "score": 3,
      "positive": "Cross-fitted support and candidate-independent deployment make the adaptation methodologically sharper and directly test the diagnosed failures.",
      "negative": "Directed transitions, recency weighting, additive fusion, shrinkage, and cross-fitting are established ingredients; this is an empirical adaptation rather than a new model family."
    },
    "future_potential": {
      "score": 6,
      "positive": "Provides an auditable framework for investigating when independent transition evidence helps current-intent ranking and when learned fusion overfits.",
      "negative": "Sparse pair coverage limits the count component, compatibility remains unobserved, and improvements under sampled evaluation may not transfer to full-catalog recommendation."
    },
    "code_difficulty": {
      "score": 5,
      "positive": "Fixed fusion uses sparse counts and a small scoring extension without external encoders or pretrained weights.",
      "negative": "Reliable cross-fitting, dense-prior preservation, diagnostic routing, exact chunked inference, and optional gating add substantial work beyond a simple residual."
    }
  },
  "related_work": [
    {
      "title": "Self-Attentive Sequential Recommendation",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1808.09781",
      "contributions": [
        "Introduces causal self-attention for sequential recommendation and supplies the starting architecture."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not explicitly require evidence of compatibility or distinguish satisfied needs from useful next purchases."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "GaGNN",
      "link": "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ins.2024.120623",
      "contributions": [
        "Combines group and individual transition graphs with collaborative and sequential encoders, motivating training-derived relational signals."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The supplied evidence does not establish improvements under the pinned RecHarness protocol.",
        "Population transitions cannot establish individual equipment ownership."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "LSIDN",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.14743",
      "contributions": [
        "Separates long-term and short-term interest modeling and uses augmentation for short-term robustness."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Transfer to sparse purchase histories and the exact four-category evaluation requires testing."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "gSASRec",
      "link": "https://www.ijcai.org/proceedings/2024/939",
      "contributions": [
        "Studies overconfidence in negative-sampled BCE and introduces generalized BCE.",
        "Motivates controlled comparisons of loss calibration and negative count."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not establish that Electronics’ reported margin weakness is caused by BCE.",
        "Improved sampled training margins need not improve validation HR@10."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Factorizing Personalized Markov Chains for Next-Basket Recommendation",
      "link": "https://archives.iw3c2.org/www2010/pub/pdfs/RendleFreudenthaler2010-FPMC.pdf",
      "contributions": [
        "Combines persistent user preferences with factorized directed transitions.",
        "Separate source and destination factors model asymmetric transitions and share evidence across pairs."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "First-order transitions omit broader context, and sparse histories weaken user-factor estimation.",
        "Establishes substantial precedent for additive preference and transition fusion."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "FOSSIL",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1609.09152",
      "contributions": [
        "Combines historical item-factor aggregation with recent sequential factors.",
        "Reduces dependence on poorly estimated user vectors in sparse settings."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Rare items and limited transition evidence remain difficult.",
        "Further limits novelty claims for combining sustained preference and recent behavior."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "SpotTarget",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.00899",
      "contributions": [
        "Identifies overfitting and distribution shift when training target edges appear in graph features.",
        "Motivates excluding a training example’s own transition evidence."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Application to supervised item-transition counts is a methodological analogy, not a demonstrated guarantee for this recommendation task."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Target Encoder’s Internal Cross Fitting",
      "link": "https://scikit-learn.org/stable/auto_examples/preprocessing/plot_target_encoder_cross_val.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Demonstrates how cross-fitting reduces overfitting from target-derived statistics.",
        "Motivates user-grouped graph construction when learning residual fusion."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "The demonstration concerns target encoding rather than sequential recommendation.",
        "User exclusion alone does not enforce global chronology or eliminate deployment distribution shift."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "Global Chronology in Offline Recommendation Evaluation",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2010.11060",
      "contributions": [
        "Shows that ignoring global chronology can change recommendation accuracy and model rankings.",
        "Motivates explicitly distinguishing user-excluded statistics from chronological feature construction."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Does not authorize changing the pinned benchmark splits.",
        "Chronological feature variants must be evaluated as separate training-feature experiments."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On the Bias of Offline Evaluation Methods for Recommender Systems",
      "link": "https://arxiv.org/abs/1912.02263",
      "contributions": [
        "Explains why sampled evaluation can change model comparisons relative to full-catalog evaluation."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Candidate-independent scoring ensures consistent item scores but cannot make sampled and full-catalog metrics equivalent."
      ]
    },
    {
      "title": "On Calibration of Modern Neural Networks",
      "link": "https://proceedings.mlr.press/v70/guo17a.html",
      "contributions": [
        "Studies temperature scaling for classification calibration.",
        "Provides context for understanding how score scaling changes a neural component’s influence in fusion."
      ],
      "limitations": [
        "Classification calibration evidence does not establish full-catalog recommendation calibration.",
        "Positive temperature scaling preserves neural ranking alone but changes fused rankings."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
